1
00:00:02,622 --> 00:00:05,080
PRESENTER: Hello everyone, and
welcome to the content video

2
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:08,320
for the Graph Data Structure.

3
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:11,020
OK, so the Graph Data
Structure, there's

4
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:13,780
some similarities between
the Tree Data Structure

5
00:00:13,780 --> 00:00:15,820
and the Graph Data Structure.

6
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:17,270
But some key differences.

7
00:00:17,270 --> 00:00:19,380
So a tree, as you can
see in my image here--

8
00:00:21,890 --> 00:00:24,710
a tree has a parent-child
relationship.

9
00:00:24,710 --> 00:00:27,260
And the nodes, or the
data that's stored,

10
00:00:27,260 --> 00:00:29,930
are connected through
these edges from parent

11
00:00:29,930 --> 00:00:33,680
to child, or from
root down to leaf.

12
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:36,830
So the graph is
similar, but the changes

13
00:00:36,830 --> 00:00:40,790
are a node can have
multiple parents.

14
00:00:40,790 --> 00:00:43,110
And you can think of it
like multiple children.

15
00:00:43,110 --> 00:00:45,980
So really the rule of one
parent to two children

16
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:47,370
doesn't exist in a graph.

17
00:00:47,370 --> 00:00:50,960
A node can be connected to
other nodes by as many edges

18
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:52,260
as you want.

19
00:00:52,260 --> 00:00:56,750
There is a concept of successor
and predecessor in graphs.

20
00:00:56,750 --> 00:01:00,380
But really, it can be--

21
00:01:00,380 --> 00:01:01,290
there's no limit.

22
00:01:01,290 --> 00:01:02,915
There's no limit to
the number of nodes

23
00:01:02,915 --> 00:01:05,290
or the number of
edges in a graph.

24
00:01:05,290 --> 00:01:07,940
Graphs are used,
really, all over.

25
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:09,550
But the common
thing between them

26
00:01:09,550 --> 00:01:12,640
all is that there
needs to be nodes,

27
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:15,550
or really where the
data pieces are stored.

28
00:01:15,550 --> 00:01:19,720
And then edges, or-- which
can be a physical connection,

29
00:01:19,720 --> 00:01:24,040
a representation of a physical
connection, or a relationship,

30
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:26,050
including a mathematical
relationship.

31
00:01:26,050 --> 00:01:29,597
But something that connects
the nodes to each other.

32
00:01:29,597 --> 00:01:31,930
As I mentioned, a graph is a
tree with multiple children

33
00:01:31,930 --> 00:01:33,310
and parents.

34
00:01:33,310 --> 00:01:35,860
And consistently,
you'll see the verbiage

35
00:01:35,860 --> 00:01:39,610
that's used when talking about
graphs is vertices and edges.

36
00:01:39,610 --> 00:01:42,050
But vertices are just
nodes-- same things.

37
00:01:42,050 --> 00:01:44,260
And I commonly use the
term nodes and edges,

38
00:01:44,260 --> 00:01:46,250
but vertices, same thing.

39
00:01:46,250 --> 00:01:47,800
So vertices and edges.

40
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:49,310
But it's a set of those.

41
00:01:49,310 --> 00:01:52,570
So a set of nodes, and then a
set of edges that connect that

42
00:01:52,570 --> 00:01:56,800
nodes in whatever way you want.

43
00:01:56,800 --> 00:01:58,510
Graphs are all over the place.

44
00:01:58,510 --> 00:02:03,880
They represent physical
things, like hardware networks,

45
00:02:03,880 --> 00:02:07,930
and/or logical things,
like this example

46
00:02:07,930 --> 00:02:12,370
of if you want to get a
degree in computer science--

47
00:02:12,370 --> 00:02:15,610
and this one's a little bit
old, but the idea the idea

48
00:02:15,610 --> 00:02:16,220
is the same.

49
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:18,400
You start here, and then you
go through all these classes,

50
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:20,942
and eventually you get down to
the bottom right of this graph

51
00:02:20,942 --> 00:02:22,940
where you graduate.

52
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:25,600
Well, the edges
represent the workflow

53
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:27,830
and then the classes
represent the nodes.

54
00:02:27,830 --> 00:02:30,450
That's the relationship.

55
00:02:30,450 --> 00:02:33,010
Now, the relationship
matters, obviously,

56
00:02:33,010 --> 00:02:35,270
because you can't take
classes in any order you want.

57
00:02:35,270 --> 00:02:37,730
There's a structure
and an ordering to it.

58
00:02:37,730 --> 00:02:39,700
So the combination of
those nodes and edges

59
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:44,290
together gives that
relationship, gives those--

60
00:02:44,290 --> 00:02:46,850
gives that structure to
the graph to represent it.

61
00:02:46,850 --> 00:02:48,040
So you start here.

62
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:51,460
You go through, take
CS1400, 1410, 2420.

63
00:02:51,460 --> 00:02:53,140
And then, as you take
those, it opens up

64
00:02:53,140 --> 00:02:56,550
the possibility of other
classes for you to take.

65
00:02:56,550 --> 00:02:59,222
You still have your prereqs over
here, your gen ed requirements.

66
00:02:59,222 --> 00:03:01,430
And then eventually, once
you've got everything done,

67
00:03:01,430 --> 00:03:05,320
you make it over to
where you graduate.

68
00:03:05,320 --> 00:03:06,380
Another common example.

69
00:03:06,380 --> 00:03:08,050
So this would be
a physical example

70
00:03:08,050 --> 00:03:11,720
that's represented with a
graph, would be bus routes.

71
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:15,640
So the edges here would
actually be a physical road,

72
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:17,570
and the nodes here
would also be physical.

73
00:03:17,570 --> 00:03:19,340
They'd be a physical bus stop.

74
00:03:19,340 --> 00:03:21,430
And the relationships,
obviously,

75
00:03:21,430 --> 00:03:25,210
are where can I
go on this route.

76
00:03:25,210 --> 00:03:27,940
So you have to think of it
in terms of the application,

77
00:03:27,940 --> 00:03:29,450
is what nodes can I get to?

78
00:03:29,450 --> 00:03:31,550
I need to go on a bus
to get to another node.

79
00:03:31,550 --> 00:03:34,240
And then, obviously,
it shows you

80
00:03:34,240 --> 00:03:36,380
the edges you're going to
traverse as you go along.

81
00:03:36,380 --> 00:03:39,910
So that's another classic
example of a graph.

82
00:03:39,910 --> 00:03:40,940
Matching algorithm.

83
00:03:40,940 --> 00:03:43,480
So this is a logical
example of a graph.

84
00:03:43,480 --> 00:03:45,700
The nodes here would be
people, and the edges

85
00:03:45,700 --> 00:03:49,540
would represent a relationship
between them, which

86
00:03:49,540 --> 00:03:53,680
could be an employee,
a job, or a dating app,

87
00:03:53,680 --> 00:03:55,370
or website that
would match people.

88
00:03:55,370 --> 00:03:57,550
The nodes would still
be people there.

89
00:03:57,550 --> 00:03:59,770
And then the edges would
be the relationship

90
00:03:59,770 --> 00:04:01,520
you're trying to match
between the people.

91
00:04:01,520 --> 00:04:07,590
So the node here is physical, a
person, and the edge is logical,

92
00:04:07,590 --> 00:04:08,640
a relationship.

93
00:04:12,580 --> 00:04:14,480
Whoop.

94
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:15,240
There we go.

95
00:04:15,240 --> 00:04:18,140
So some other terms when you're
dealing with graph-- and this

96
00:04:18,140 --> 00:04:20,570
really sets up when we talk
about traversing a graph,

97
00:04:20,570 --> 00:04:22,880
or how to get through
graphs the fastest,

98
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:26,270
or how to calculate things
efficiently to make decisions

99
00:04:26,270 --> 00:04:27,690
with the data in a graph.

100
00:04:27,690 --> 00:04:30,920
Here's some terminology
and some concepts

101
00:04:30,920 --> 00:04:32,730
we're going to need for those.

102
00:04:32,730 --> 00:04:35,090
So graph, we mentioned, is
a set of vertices and edges

103
00:04:35,090 --> 00:04:36,020
connected with--

104
00:04:36,020 --> 00:04:39,860
or, excuse me, of vertices
and nodes connected by edges.

105
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:43,670
Commonly, when you see
mathematical representations--

106
00:04:43,670 --> 00:04:44,960
you know, the--

107
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:48,380
it'll be V and E.
This relationship

108
00:04:48,380 --> 00:04:50,690
of successor-predecessor,
it's similar to a tree

109
00:04:50,690 --> 00:04:53,480
structure of parent-child,
but not necessarily.

110
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:55,520
Successor and
predecessor, now, more

111
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:58,230
has to do with the way
you traverse a graph.

112
00:04:58,230 --> 00:05:01,190
So for the example of getting
a computer science degree,

113
00:05:01,190 --> 00:05:04,580
there's an obvious flow there
of successor-predecessor,.

114
00:05:04,580 --> 00:05:06,825
But that not necessarily--
that might not necessarily

115
00:05:06,825 --> 00:05:07,950
be the case in every graph.

116
00:05:07,950 --> 00:05:11,820
It might be a
logical relationship,

117
00:05:11,820 --> 00:05:13,820
where you could go-- a
predecessor could go back

118
00:05:13,820 --> 00:05:15,330
and visit a successor again.

119
00:05:15,330 --> 00:05:19,602
Maybe to try and use that
getting a degree example,

120
00:05:19,602 --> 00:05:22,060
Maybe you have to go back and
take a class for some reason.

121
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:24,630
That would be like going
back to the successor.

122
00:05:24,630 --> 00:05:25,560
And then degrees.

123
00:05:25,560 --> 00:05:29,010
Degrees are the number
of edges each node has

124
00:05:29,010 --> 00:05:30,550
or each vertex has.

125
00:05:30,550 --> 00:05:32,490
The indegree is the
number of edges coming in,

126
00:05:32,490 --> 00:05:36,130
and the outdegree is the
number of edges going out.

127
00:05:36,130 --> 00:05:37,690
There's another
relationship we need

128
00:05:37,690 --> 00:05:40,060
to cover when
talking about graphs,

129
00:05:40,060 --> 00:05:43,210
is directed versus undirected.

130
00:05:43,210 --> 00:05:48,100
So directed refers to
when an edge has a--

131
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:49,520
an edge is an arrow.

132
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:51,740
An edge has a flow to it.

133
00:05:51,740 --> 00:05:54,490
It has a--

134
00:05:54,490 --> 00:05:59,860
It cannot be-- an edge going
from node A to node B does not

135
00:05:59,860 --> 00:06:01,480
necessarily, in
a directed graph,

136
00:06:01,480 --> 00:06:08,350
mean there's an edge back from
B to A. So the direction matters

137
00:06:08,350 --> 00:06:09,490
in a directed graph.

138
00:06:09,490 --> 00:06:11,950
Undirected is there's
no concept of that.

139
00:06:11,950 --> 00:06:16,090
Any edge-- like, for example,
the matching algorithm--

140
00:06:16,090 --> 00:06:19,750
if you're trying to match
people in a dating app,

141
00:06:19,750 --> 00:06:24,080
if they're a match, then that
just means an edge exists.

142
00:06:24,080 --> 00:06:27,220
There's a relationship or
a possible relationship

143
00:06:27,220 --> 00:06:31,510
between these two people,
whereas maybe in a network flow,

144
00:06:31,510 --> 00:06:35,840
or back to the bus
routes-- let me put that

145
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:37,620
in really quick-- traffic flow.

146
00:06:37,620 --> 00:06:39,540
Traffic flow could
kind of be both.

147
00:06:39,540 --> 00:06:42,620
So a-- traffic could
be an undirected graph

148
00:06:42,620 --> 00:06:45,740
if, for the roads that
are represented by edges,

149
00:06:45,740 --> 00:06:48,000
there's flow to
and from the nodes.

150
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:50,060
So both-- two-way traffic.

151
00:06:50,060 --> 00:06:51,260
You can go both ways.

152
00:06:51,260 --> 00:06:53,120
However, if there's
a one-way street,

153
00:06:53,120 --> 00:06:54,390
then that would be directed.

154
00:06:54,390 --> 00:06:56,220
You can only go one direction.

155
00:06:56,220 --> 00:07:00,560
So that edge would
have a direction to it.

156
00:07:00,560 --> 00:07:02,820
And then the terminology
is a little bit different.

157
00:07:02,820 --> 00:07:05,660
So indegree for directed graph,
it's indegree and outdegree,

158
00:07:05,660 --> 00:07:08,272
which-- the number of edges
coming in and leaving.

159
00:07:08,272 --> 00:07:09,980
But if it's undirected,
where there's not

160
00:07:09,980 --> 00:07:14,450
the concept of in and out,
it's just called degree--

161
00:07:14,450 --> 00:07:17,000
degree and neighbors--
when talking

162
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,310
about an undirected graph.

163
00:07:19,310 --> 00:07:21,790
OK, so younger than.

164
00:07:21,790 --> 00:07:24,840
So you tell me what kind of
graph is this going to be,

165
00:07:24,840 --> 00:07:27,250
directed or undirected?

166
00:07:27,250 --> 00:07:28,925
Well, one pretty big hint.

167
00:07:28,925 --> 00:07:30,550
You can go look at
the graph right away

168
00:07:30,550 --> 00:07:31,750
and see that it has arrows.

169
00:07:31,750 --> 00:07:34,810
So you know that it's directed.

170
00:07:34,810 --> 00:07:37,250
But they're arrows, though.

171
00:07:37,250 --> 00:07:40,100
It's not directed
because we have arrows.

172
00:07:40,100 --> 00:07:42,380
It's because the
relationship is directed.

173
00:07:42,380 --> 00:07:44,080
So in this example,
it's a younger than.

174
00:07:44,080 --> 00:07:46,430
Point to someone who's
younger than you.

175
00:07:46,430 --> 00:07:48,310
Well, that right
there is you need

176
00:07:48,310 --> 00:07:52,280
to determine the relationship
of who is younger than me.

177
00:07:52,280 --> 00:07:54,138
That brings in a direction.

178
00:07:54,138 --> 00:07:55,430
That person is younger than me.

179
00:07:55,430 --> 00:07:56,420
That person is older than me.

180
00:07:56,420 --> 00:07:58,450
I'm not going to point to them,
but they can point back to me.

181
00:07:58,450 --> 00:07:59,840
That's a directed graph.

182
00:07:59,840 --> 00:08:01,300
So even though
it's not physical,

183
00:08:01,300 --> 00:08:03,735
it's still the relationship
determines it's directed.

184
00:08:06,880 --> 00:08:08,080
OK.

185
00:08:08,080 --> 00:08:09,700
Another example of a graph--

186
00:08:09,700 --> 00:08:13,810
and this is kind of setting up
how you store a graph, what,

187
00:08:13,810 --> 00:08:16,210
what data structure, what
data types, and the structure

188
00:08:16,210 --> 00:08:17,990
of a graph looks like
when you store it.

189
00:08:17,990 --> 00:08:20,240
So here's an example of
someone you want to work with.

190
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:22,150
You can look at the vertices.

191
00:08:22,150 --> 00:08:24,040
So here, it'll be a set.

192
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:26,320
Notice the squirrely braces.

193
00:08:26,320 --> 00:08:30,370
Although it's not really a
dictionary, this is kind of--

194
00:08:30,370 --> 00:08:35,740
I'm using-- this slide refers to
the mathematic notation of it,

195
00:08:35,740 --> 00:08:37,490
not necessarily the Python code.

196
00:08:37,490 --> 00:08:38,919
So here's all the edge--

197
00:08:38,919 --> 00:08:40,580
0, 1, 2, 3, 4.

198
00:08:40,580 --> 00:08:45,050
And then the-- excuse me, here's
all the nodes, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4.

199
00:08:45,050 --> 00:08:48,260
And then the edges, notice
they're always in pairs--

200
00:08:48,260 --> 00:08:51,830
0,1; 1,2; 0,3; 3,0; 2,2; 4,3.

201
00:08:51,830 --> 00:08:56,270
So we can go down here and see
all of those edges are directed.

202
00:08:56,270 --> 00:08:59,470
So here's all of the
arrows between the nodes.

203
00:08:59,470 --> 00:09:01,070
And what about this
loop here, 2,2?

204
00:09:01,070 --> 00:09:03,450
Well, we have an
edge from 2 to 2.

205
00:09:03,450 --> 00:09:05,810
So that would be a loop.

206
00:09:05,810 --> 00:09:07,570
That's exactly what
a loop is in a graph,

207
00:09:07,570 --> 00:09:09,910
is when you have one
node, have an edge that

208
00:09:09,910 --> 00:09:11,793
comes back to itself.

209
00:09:11,793 --> 00:09:13,210
And that could
represent something

210
00:09:13,210 --> 00:09:16,030
in the physical
world, like a park

211
00:09:16,030 --> 00:09:20,650
up in the mountains
where a road from a cabin

212
00:09:20,650 --> 00:09:22,330
goes around to see
something beautiful,

213
00:09:22,330 --> 00:09:23,997
and it just comes
back around to itself.

214
00:09:23,997 --> 00:09:26,320
And that's all the road
does, is loop around

215
00:09:26,320 --> 00:09:28,610
so you can see the
beautiful environment.

216
00:09:28,610 --> 00:09:31,670
That could be an example
of a loop inside a graph.

217
00:09:31,670 --> 00:09:35,760
But yeah, there it is.

218
00:09:35,760 --> 00:09:38,850
And, as I mentioned,
this is really commonly

219
00:09:38,850 --> 00:09:41,500
what you're going to use
graphs for in the real world

220
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:45,600
in your job one day, is graphs
that represent relationships.

221
00:09:45,600 --> 00:09:50,430
The node x likes y, like if it's
a customer and they're shopping,

222
00:09:50,430 --> 00:09:52,500
what's a product
they might like?

223
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:55,190
x knows how to contact
y in a contact list.

224
00:09:55,190 --> 00:09:58,170
A friend of y, x, wants to work
for y-- like a LinkedIn kind

225
00:09:58,170 --> 00:09:59,280
of scenario there.

226
00:09:59,280 --> 00:10:02,772
x is a child of y.

227
00:10:02,772 --> 00:10:04,230
If you're thinking,
well, I thought

228
00:10:04,230 --> 00:10:08,050
we left child and parent
structures with trees,

229
00:10:08,050 --> 00:10:08,550
well yeah.

230
00:10:08,550 --> 00:10:10,110
But you can just
make a child edge,

231
00:10:10,110 --> 00:10:11,820
and then this edge
represents a child,

232
00:10:11,820 --> 00:10:12,840
and start using those edges.

233
00:10:12,840 --> 00:10:14,340
And then you've,
you've brought back

234
00:10:14,340 --> 00:10:15,730
in the parent-child
relationship.

235
00:10:15,730 --> 00:10:18,480
There's a flight
from x to x to y.

236
00:10:18,480 --> 00:10:20,890
Clubs x and y share common
members, things like that.

237
00:10:20,890 --> 00:10:24,610
So there's countless
examples of relationships.

238
00:10:24,610 --> 00:10:26,070
And when you start
to think about--

239
00:10:26,070 --> 00:10:28,350
you know, that's really
what graphs are used for,

240
00:10:28,350 --> 00:10:32,640
is when you have a system
where the relationships get

241
00:10:32,640 --> 00:10:37,980
complex, a list, a dictionary,
a table, and a database

242
00:10:37,980 --> 00:10:39,580
doesn't quite capture it all.

243
00:10:39,580 --> 00:10:42,630
And you need
something to capture

244
00:10:42,630 --> 00:10:45,360
both the data itself
and the relationship

245
00:10:45,360 --> 00:10:47,100
with other pieces of data.

246
00:10:47,100 --> 00:10:49,290
A graph is perfect for that.

247
00:10:49,290 --> 00:10:49,790
OK.

248
00:10:49,790 --> 00:10:52,280
And I threw some examples.

249
00:10:52,280 --> 00:10:55,700
I put into ChatGPT, "What are
some examples of the real world

250
00:10:55,700 --> 00:10:58,380
of graphs used today?"

